00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:32.960
yesterday.

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:35.240
She mentioned that
one of her friends

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:39.650
was doing wildlife
preservation in the oceans.

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:43.010
And today, we are bringing
Professor Janet Mann

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:48.080
onto the stage to share with
us the story of her work,

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:50.530
collaborating with
Google Cloud AI,

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:54.240
on preserving wildlife in ocean.

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:55.786
Welcome, Professor Janet.

00:29:55.786 --> 00:29:57.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:00.912 --> 00:30:02.097
JANET MANN: Oh, I need that.

00:30:02.097 --> 00:30:06.710
[LAUGHS] Thank you.

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:09.400
Thank you, it's a
delight to be here.

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:11.920
So you just heard about
identifying species

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.210
using AutoML.

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:16.630
I'm going to talk about
identifying individual animals,

00:30:16.630 --> 00:30:19.840
tracking wildlife using ML.

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:26.890
So tracking individuals is
a critical part of biology

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:30.430
because natural selection
acts on the variation

00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:31.540
between individuals.

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:34.060
It's also important for
conservation and management.

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:37.390
And it also appeals
to the public,

00:30:37.390 --> 00:30:42.400
in terms of their attraction
to individual animals

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:43.730
and interest in them.

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:46.780
A poignant and sad example
that you are probably

00:30:46.780 --> 00:30:51.400
all familiar with was the Free
Willy, also known as Keiko,

00:30:51.400 --> 00:30:55.810
where people spent over $20
million on a lost cause,

00:30:55.810 --> 00:30:59.620
really, to introduce
him to the wild.

00:30:59.620 --> 00:31:02.710
At the same time,
the killer whales off

00:31:02.710 --> 00:31:05.980
the coast of Washington
State and British Columbia

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:09.650
have been declining and are
on the verge of extinction.

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:13.610
So you can use the
individual to engage people.

00:31:13.610 --> 00:31:16.370
So this was an example
that wasn't very good,

00:31:16.370 --> 00:31:20.790
but it shows how important
the individual is

00:31:20.790 --> 00:31:26.200
in understanding these animals.

00:31:26.200 --> 00:31:29.430
So there's lots of species that
can be tracked through time--

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:33.030
chimpanzees by their
faces, zebras by stripes,

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:35.760
and cheetah by the
stripes on their tails,

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:40.270
manta rays by the spots on
their bellies, and whale sharks

00:31:40.270 --> 00:31:44.580
also by their spot patterns,
and even toads by the gland

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:48.720
markings on the Wyoming
toad on their backs.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.880
So these are individually
distinctive, but stable

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:52.250
through time.

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:56.020
How about individuals
that change through time?

00:31:56.020 --> 00:31:58.780
So elephants, they are
identified by their ears.

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:01.290
And the tears
change through time.

00:32:01.290 --> 00:32:03.070
Sperm whales by
their tail flukes--

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:06.570
and actually, most whale
species by their flukes.

00:32:06.570 --> 00:32:08.640
And those patterns
change through time.

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:13.380
And Risso's dolphins by the
scarring and dorsal fins.

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:16.620
So for over 30 years,
I've been studying

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:18.480
dolphins in Western Australia.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:20.160
Pretty far, remote place.

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:22.830
And we've been tracking
their residential population.

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:26.160
And we've been tracking over
1,700 individuals from birth

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:26.965
to death.

00:32:26.965 --> 00:32:27.840
They just live there.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:30.150
This is Bytefluke.

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:32.030
And as you can see,
her fin has changed.

00:32:32.030 --> 00:32:33.930
It is called Shark
Bay where I work.

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:37.050
And so the dolphins do
get bitten up by sharks,

00:32:37.050 --> 00:32:40.660
and their fins do
change over time.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:42.360
Bytefluke, by the
way, 10 years ago

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:44.850
had a calf that we named Google.

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:47.460
And Google is still
alive and well.

00:32:47.460 --> 00:32:53.430
He's 10 years old, figuring
out who his friends are.

00:32:53.430 --> 00:32:56.640
And then, here's Zombie,
a daughter of Phantom,

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:57.830
born in 2011.

00:32:57.830 --> 00:33:02.490
And as you can see, Zombie's
fin changed quite dramatically

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:04.440
in four years.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:06.690
So we know these animals,
and we can track them.

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:10.980
And basically, how do you track
animals that you don't know?

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:14.470
So I'm going to take you to
another part of the world,

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:19.680
but first I want to say,
we gave tens of thousands

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:24.000
of photographs to the
Google AutoML team

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.580
to help us build something
that could track animals

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:28.800
that we know a lot less about.

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:30.794
So that was the
training data set.

00:33:30.794 --> 00:33:32.960
And so take it to the other
side of the world-- trhe

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:34.050
Potomac Chesapeake.

00:33:34.050 --> 00:33:36.060
Yes, there are dolphins
in the Potomac.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:37.860
And because it is
the Potomac, we've

00:33:37.860 --> 00:33:41.220
named them after presidents,
vice presidents, founding

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:43.250
fathers, first families.

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:46.260
So this is Hubert
Humphrey, who we've seen

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:51.010
for three years in the Potomac.

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:55.750
And to find out where
these animals go,

00:33:55.750 --> 00:33:58.900
because they're only there for
about six months of the year,

00:33:58.900 --> 00:34:00.280
we want to see
where they winter.

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:04.150
And fortunately, scientists
have been collecting dorsal fin

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:08.449
photographs from animals all
over the Western Atlantic.

00:34:08.449 --> 00:34:11.380
And so this is put together
in the Mid-Atlantic,

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:14.020
Bottlenose Dolphin Catalog.

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:16.540
And so our challenge
was, could we

00:34:16.540 --> 00:34:19.570
match the Potomac
Chesapeake dolphins

00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:21.070
to these other locations.

00:34:21.070 --> 00:34:24.780
So I'm going to show you
an example, using AutoML.

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:26.670
And this way, we could
get critical problems

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:27.378
in their biology.

00:34:27.378 --> 00:34:32.040
So this is George
Mason, who was one

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:33.840
of the key authors in
the Bill of Rights,

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:35.650
in case you're interested.

00:34:35.650 --> 00:34:42.165
But he was photographed in
the Potomac in 2015, 2017.

00:34:42.165 --> 00:34:43.290
This is what it looks like.

00:34:43.290 --> 00:34:47.570
So we ran the image
through AutoML,

00:34:47.570 --> 00:34:51.350
and we tested it against the
Mid-Atlantic Bottlenose Dolphin

00:34:51.350 --> 00:34:53.270
Catalog.

00:34:53.270 --> 00:34:56.960
And as you can see here,
the first two that came up

00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:01.520
were actually George Mason.

00:35:01.520 --> 00:35:05.480
And he was seen off the
coast of North Carolina

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:07.850
in 2008 and 2010.

00:35:07.850 --> 00:35:12.390
So this is a 10-year
difference, practically,

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:13.730
from when this animal was seen.

00:35:13.730 --> 00:35:16.700
And this is how
effective this has been.

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:20.990
So Google AutoML has enabled us
to identify individual dolphins

00:35:20.990 --> 00:35:22.310
in seconds.

00:35:22.310 --> 00:35:25.669
And to do this-- we usually do
our matching by eye for animals

00:35:25.669 --> 00:35:26.210
that we know.

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:28.220
But for animals
that we don't know,

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:31.580
we can use this
technology to map them

00:35:31.580 --> 00:35:34.520
onto animals in other
locations and track

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:35.930
migrating individuals.

00:35:35.930 --> 00:35:38.840
And this informs us a
lot about the populations

00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:40.940
for conservation purposes.

00:35:40.940 --> 00:35:45.140
So I want to thank Google
AutoML for helping us with this.

00:35:45.140 --> 00:35:48.810
And I'm going to give the
stage back to Fei-Fei.

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:49.310
Thank you.

00:35:49.310 --> 00:35:50.518
FEI-FEI LI: Thank you, Janet.

00:35:50.518 --> 00:35:52.635
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:55.485 --> 00:35:58.610
That is just amazing.

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:00.140
It's not the first
time I saw it,

00:36:00.140 --> 00:36:05.950
but my heart is beating when
looking at the amazing result.

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:11.570
So we spent the past two
days sharing with you

00:36:11.570 --> 00:36:17.330
our excitement about how AI
can empower industries, make

00:36:17.330 --> 00:36:20.660
life work better, when
it's applied right.

00:36:20.660 --> 00:36:24.260
But the power does
demand responsibility.

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:27.800
And it is our collective
responsibility

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:30.920
to make this a
benevolent technology.

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:35.450
Earlier this year, Google
published AI principles

00:36:35.450 --> 00:36:39.275
to guide our company's
work and development of AI

00:36:39.275 --> 00:36:43.490
to do the things that
we believe are important

00:36:43.490 --> 00:36:46.560
and adhere to our values.

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:50.480
So today, I'm going
to welcome onstage

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:56.090
two colleagues of mine who will
share a conversation with you,

00:36:56.090 --> 00:37:03.110
with us, about how we can
develop responsible AI,

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:06.740
both at Google, as
well as inviting you

00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:11.990
to this dialogue about why
it's important to all of us

00:37:11.990 --> 00:37:16.800
in the enterprise business
about developing responsible AI

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:17.610
technology.

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:20.542
So please, Tracy and Shannon.

00:37:20.542 --> 00:37:22.952
