00:39:09.770 --> 00:39:12.450
So we don't have a
whole lot of time,

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:15.920
but we're going to have a
conversation here with you

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:19.520
and hope to open that
conversation for many years

00:39:19.520 --> 00:39:23.630
to come in this journey
together about the importance

00:39:23.630 --> 00:39:24.470
of this topic.

00:39:24.470 --> 00:39:27.920
So I'm going to
start with Shannon.

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:34.010
Why does ethics of AI matter
to any company, including

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:35.289
enterprise companies?

00:39:35.289 --> 00:39:36.830
SHANNON VALOR: Well,
I think you have

00:39:36.830 --> 00:39:40.190
to recognize that
public trust is

00:39:40.190 --> 00:39:44.330
one of the most important things
that any corporation needs

00:39:44.330 --> 00:39:46.370
to think about, needs to earn.

00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:50.510
And public trust in technology
is on a shaky foundation

00:39:50.510 --> 00:39:51.710
these days.

00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:53.990
And so lots of
companies are having

00:39:53.990 --> 00:39:57.200
to think about, OK,
well, how do we cultivate

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:02.100
and build up that public trust
in technology in a way that's

00:40:02.100 --> 00:40:03.800
sincere and earned?

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:06.680
And I think that one
of the things that we

00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:09.710
have to recognize is that
we've been, for a while,

00:40:09.710 --> 00:40:14.030
in a reactive mode where we
see ethical issues that crop up

00:40:14.030 --> 00:40:15.582
in society as a
result of the way

00:40:15.582 --> 00:40:17.540
certain technologies are
developed or deployed,

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:19.550
and then we jump in
and try to fix it.

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:21.410
But what we really
have to do is step back

00:40:21.410 --> 00:40:24.382
and start getting into a more
anticipatory mode where we're

00:40:24.382 --> 00:40:26.090
seeing the road ahead
a little bit, where

00:40:26.090 --> 00:40:28.670
we're thinking about where
the problems will crop up.

00:40:28.670 --> 00:40:30.740
And we do this in all
other kinds of engineering,

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:33.380
and technology, and development
with other kinds of issues,

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:36.710
thinking ahead about where you
might have some stress points

00:40:36.710 --> 00:40:38.760
or where you might have
some complications.

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:41.240
So now, we just have to
bring the ethical expertise

00:40:41.240 --> 00:40:42.680
into that process.

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:43.430
FEI-FEI LI: Great.

00:40:43.430 --> 00:40:46.280
Yeah, so turn a
reactive understanding

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:50.270
to a proactive
action of embedding

00:40:50.270 --> 00:40:53.330
ethical and responsible
principles into the AI

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:54.770
and technology.

00:40:54.770 --> 00:40:58.220
So to go a little more
concrete, because we

00:40:58.220 --> 00:41:00.510
want to actually turn
that into action,

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:03.830
what are the key ethical
principles for an enterprise

00:41:03.830 --> 00:41:07.520
company who is practicing AI?

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:09.920
SHANNON VALOR: Well, I think
one of the things you have

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:11.870
to think about are what are
going to be your stated goals

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:12.560
and values?

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:14.420
And right now, I
think, especially

00:41:14.420 --> 00:41:16.400
given where the
public trust is right

00:41:16.400 --> 00:41:19.610
now and public confidence, I
think fairness and transparency

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:23.120
have to be two of the stated
goals of any enterprise,

00:41:23.120 --> 00:41:26.840
of any organization that wants
to earn the public trust.

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:29.360
And then, I think you also
have to think about values

00:41:29.360 --> 00:41:32.720
like safety and security, which
sometimes we forget that those

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:34.730
are moral values, right?

00:41:34.730 --> 00:41:36.410
Safety and security
are about protecting

00:41:36.410 --> 00:41:38.790
things that matter for people.

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:42.120
And so thinking about those as
values that we're committed to

00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:44.390
and that we have systems
in place to preserve

00:41:44.390 --> 00:41:46.105
is really important.

00:41:46.105 --> 00:41:47.480
I think we also
have to recognize

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:49.760
that the other kinds of
attributes that we might have

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:52.160
in play in our decisions,
even things we often

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:55.220
think of as just mechanical
attributes, like speed,

00:41:55.220 --> 00:41:59.780
or precision, or efficiency,
that these are values too.

00:41:59.780 --> 00:42:02.810
And we have to have them
in the same conversation

00:42:02.810 --> 00:42:05.480
with the decisions we're
making about the ethical values

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:07.365
that we're entrusted
with protecting.

00:42:07.365 --> 00:42:09.740
Because often, there are some
very challenging trade-offs

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:12.634
that happen between values,
like speed, and efficiency,

00:42:12.634 --> 00:42:14.300
and precision, and
other kinds of things

00:42:14.300 --> 00:42:17.702
we care about, like fairness,
transparency, and so on.

00:42:17.702 --> 00:42:19.160
So I think
understanding that there

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:21.620
are no ethically
neutral decisions that

00:42:21.620 --> 00:42:24.560
get made when you're talking
about technologies that shape

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:28.670
people's lives, that influence
the institutions in society,

00:42:28.670 --> 00:42:30.410
there's no way to
be ethically neutral

00:42:30.410 --> 00:42:34.310
or to detach yourself from
that set of questions.

00:42:34.310 --> 00:42:37.180
So you have to just dive
in and try to get it right.

00:42:37.180 --> 00:42:38.264
FEI-FEI LI: To embrace it.

00:42:38.264 --> 00:42:39.346
SHANNON VALOR: Absolutely.

00:42:39.346 --> 00:42:40.100
FEI-FEI LI: Yeah.

00:42:40.100 --> 00:42:45.080
So actually, speaking of
practicing these ethical AI

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:50.450
principles, so Tracy, we
rolled out AI principles

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:52.460
earlier this year.

00:42:52.460 --> 00:42:56.120
And we together, and
many of our colleagues,

00:42:56.120 --> 00:42:58.670
are working hard to
make responsibility

00:42:58.670 --> 00:43:00.650
a part of our process.

00:43:00.650 --> 00:43:03.740
So I would love for
you to share with us

00:43:03.740 --> 00:43:09.050
and share with our partners
and enterprise colleagues

00:43:09.050 --> 00:43:10.822
what we are doing.

00:43:10.822 --> 00:43:11.780
TRACY FREY: Yeah, sure.

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:13.670
And a lot of it is a
lot of what Shannon

00:43:13.670 --> 00:43:15.680
was saying around
starting at the beginning,

00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:18.600
and how do we think about
it from the earliest stages?

00:43:18.600 --> 00:43:24.170
So within Cloud AI, we've really
taken the responsible use of AI

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:27.675
and made it a goal of
our development process.

00:43:27.675 --> 00:43:30.230
And alongside the
AI principles--

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:33.360
that I hope everyone's had
a chance to read, if not,

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:37.310
they're certainly easy
to find on our website--

00:43:37.310 --> 00:43:39.020
but alongside those,
we actually did

00:43:39.020 --> 00:43:43.250
our own human-centered approach
within Cloud AI to say,

00:43:43.250 --> 00:43:45.062
what are the core
values of this group?

00:43:45.062 --> 00:43:46.520
These are the set
of humans who are

00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:49.290
going to be creating this
technology to use at scale,

00:43:49.290 --> 00:43:51.762
so what does this group
care about the most?

00:43:51.762 --> 00:43:52.970
What do we think is critical?

00:43:52.970 --> 00:43:59.220
And what came out of that was
courage, integrity, compassion,

00:43:59.220 --> 00:44:00.600
and impact.

00:44:00.600 --> 00:44:04.220
Now, given how rapidly
this market is expanding--

00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:06.020
I mean, just even
the size of this room

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:08.750
or the size of this conference,
the level of interest

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:11.660
that we see and how
quickly it's being used--

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:14.720
given that and the fact
that many customers really

00:44:14.720 --> 00:44:18.520
need to use their own data as a
part of any deployed solution,

00:44:18.520 --> 00:44:21.740
well, that really makes
the assessing of fairness

00:44:21.740 --> 00:44:25.190
and ethical impact,
especially at scale

00:44:25.190 --> 00:44:29.610
of technology in enterprises,
it makes it really complex.

00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:31.700
And so what we've
done is, we've really

00:44:31.700 --> 00:44:36.230
committed to evaluating the
work of Cloud AI carefully

00:44:36.230 --> 00:44:39.590
and really in depth, with
the goal of aligning it

00:44:39.590 --> 00:44:42.800
with those principles, from the
time the development starts,

00:44:42.800 --> 00:44:44.960
all the way through
to hearing back

00:44:44.960 --> 00:44:48.780
from our customers and partners
when it's out in the world.

00:44:48.780 --> 00:44:51.150
FEI-FEI LI: So can
you give us an example

00:44:51.150 --> 00:44:58.782
that a current Cloud AI product
that has reflected this?

00:44:58.782 --> 00:44:59.490
TRACY FREY: Yeah.

00:44:59.490 --> 00:45:03.260
And certainly, I think in terms
of where it is in our product,

00:45:03.260 --> 00:45:06.420
we're still at the very
beginning of a lot of this.

00:45:06.420 --> 00:45:09.260
But even in AutoML
for example, we

00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:12.080
worked really hard to create
an inclusive machine learning

00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:15.620
guide that's available now
to all of our customers

00:45:15.620 --> 00:45:16.880
who are accessing AutoML.

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:18.940
And we worked hard to
build it into the product.

00:45:18.940 --> 00:45:21.410
So as you're building
the model, you

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:23.330
can then reference that guide.

00:45:23.330 --> 00:45:26.596
And it's infused in our
documentation, as well.

00:45:26.596 --> 00:45:28.220
And then eventually,
we're working hard

00:45:28.220 --> 00:45:33.840
on tools and testing of our
own models, to transparency,

00:45:33.840 --> 00:45:36.170
and all the kinds of things
that we can offer to you,

00:45:36.170 --> 00:45:39.120
the kinds of tools that we can
build to give to our customers

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:41.570
around how they can
assess their own models

00:45:41.570 --> 00:45:44.570
and think about what is
in their data already

00:45:44.570 --> 00:45:46.882
and how to think about it
from an ethical standpoint.

00:45:46.882 --> 00:45:47.590
FEI-FEI LI: Yeah.

00:45:47.590 --> 00:45:50.885
One thing-- I think we all
share this experience--

00:45:50.885 --> 00:45:53.600
AI is a very nascent technology.

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:57.560
But even with its
nascency, the importance

00:45:57.560 --> 00:46:02.780
of fairness and responsible
development and uses

00:46:02.780 --> 00:46:08.700
is already very much
important in our products

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:10.770
and on the mind of many people.

00:46:10.770 --> 00:46:13.580
So even at Google,
a lot of these

00:46:13.580 --> 00:46:17.350
are the first steps we're
taking to make responsible AI

00:46:17.350 --> 00:46:17.870
products.

00:46:17.870 --> 00:46:21.200
So I want to ask,
back to Shannon,

00:46:21.200 --> 00:46:23.780
that you talk about
public trust--

00:46:23.780 --> 00:46:29.740
public trust is not something
that should be a goal.

00:46:29.740 --> 00:46:34.200
It should be a consequence of
what we do, that we earn that.

00:46:34.200 --> 00:46:35.810
You said that.

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:39.080
So you heard from Tracy
that Google is already

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:41.760
starting to take steps on that.

00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:43.310
How do we sustain this?

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:45.710
How do we sustain
our AI principles?

00:46:45.710 --> 00:46:50.180
And how do we earn this
long-term, especially

00:46:50.180 --> 00:46:54.870
technology like AI and many
others changes rapidly?

00:46:54.870 --> 00:46:55.990
SHANNON VALOR: Yeah.

00:46:55.990 --> 00:46:58.520
And I think that's the key
is to understand that this

00:46:58.520 --> 00:47:01.490
is a long game, and
not something that

00:47:01.490 --> 00:47:05.870
has a quick, easy technical fix,
or a quick, easy social fix.

00:47:05.870 --> 00:47:07.370
And it's also
important to recognize

00:47:07.370 --> 00:47:10.730
that technologies evolve,
people evolve with them.

00:47:10.730 --> 00:47:14.210
Technologies also influence
other technologies,

00:47:14.210 --> 00:47:16.010
interact in surprising
ways sometimes

00:47:16.010 --> 00:47:17.360
with other technologies.

00:47:17.360 --> 00:47:20.430
It's a complex web that's
constantly shifting,

00:47:20.430 --> 00:47:22.910
which means that
to manage what's

00:47:22.910 --> 00:47:25.460
going on in that web
in a responsible way,

00:47:25.460 --> 00:47:26.870
you have to always be there.

00:47:26.870 --> 00:47:28.910
And you have to always
be paying attention.

00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:31.430
And you have to be
constantly seeing this

00:47:31.430 --> 00:47:34.100
as an unending task,
something that you're not

00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:36.980
looking to finish and tie a bow
on, turn your back on, and walk

00:47:36.980 --> 00:47:38.870
away, but something
that you're expecting

00:47:38.870 --> 00:47:42.050
that the field of interaction
between technology,

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:45.890
and society, and people is
always going to be shifting.

00:47:45.890 --> 00:47:48.920
And managing that
in a trustworthy way

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:52.316
is something that will never
stop being our responsibility.

00:47:52.316 --> 00:47:53.365
FEI-FEI LI: Yeah.

00:47:53.365 --> 00:47:54.080
That's great.

00:47:54.080 --> 00:47:57.320
So one last question, Shannon.

00:47:57.320 --> 00:48:02.100
So you've been an academician
for most of your life

00:48:02.100 --> 00:48:06.200
and working on books, and
theories, and philosophies

00:48:06.200 --> 00:48:09.110
of technology ethics.

00:48:09.110 --> 00:48:13.520
But now, you're taking a
step into the industry world

00:48:13.520 --> 00:48:17.795
and putting all your expertise
and knowledge into practice.

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:21.537
Does that excite you?

00:48:21.537 --> 00:48:22.370
SHANNON VALOR: Yeah.

00:48:22.370 --> 00:48:24.020
FEI-FEI LI: What do
you feel about it?

00:48:24.020 --> 00:48:24.853
SHANNON VALOR: Sure.

00:48:24.853 --> 00:48:27.860
Well, I've always been more of
a practice-oriented philosopher

00:48:27.860 --> 00:48:29.190
than a theoretician.

00:48:29.190 --> 00:48:32.470
That's partly why I came
around to study these topics.

00:48:32.470 --> 00:48:34.730
FEI-FEI LI: I didn't
know philosophers are all

00:48:34.730 --> 00:48:35.480
practice-oriented.

00:48:35.480 --> 00:48:37.896
SHANNON VALOR: We do exist,
practice-oriented philosophers

00:48:37.896 --> 00:48:38.751
do exist.

00:48:38.751 --> 00:48:40.750
There may be a little
fewer than they should be,

00:48:40.750 --> 00:48:42.089
but we're out there.

00:48:42.089 --> 00:48:44.380
But you know, I've been
teaching engineers and computer

00:48:44.380 --> 00:48:48.310
scientists for over a
decade about these issues

00:48:48.310 --> 00:48:50.650
and teaching working
engineers, even,

00:48:50.650 --> 00:48:53.320
about how to develop
ethical principles

00:48:53.320 --> 00:48:56.920
and apply them in different
professional contexts.

00:48:56.920 --> 00:48:58.750
Most of my research
partners are people

00:48:58.750 --> 00:49:02.330
who are involved in engineering
and computer science, as well.

00:49:02.330 --> 00:49:05.000
So it's an area that
I'm very comfortable in.

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:07.300
But in general, I also
think that if philosophers

00:49:07.300 --> 00:49:09.760
aren't available to step up
when society needs them, then

00:49:09.760 --> 00:49:11.110
what good are we?

00:49:11.110 --> 00:49:12.694
So I'm kind of with
Plato on that one.

00:49:12.694 --> 00:49:14.859
Those of you who might have
read "Plato's Republic",

00:49:14.859 --> 00:49:17.440
there's this whole thing about
when society needs your help,

00:49:17.440 --> 00:49:21.250
you have to put the book down
and step up and do the work.

00:49:21.250 --> 00:49:23.625
So I'm excited to be part
of Google's work now.

00:49:23.625 --> 00:49:25.000
FEI-FEI LI: Well,
I can tell you,

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:27.550
we are all excited to
be working with you

00:49:27.550 --> 00:49:29.710
and getting your advice.

00:49:29.710 --> 00:49:35.020
Like you said, it's a long
run, and it's a journey

00:49:35.020 --> 00:49:36.710
we're going to share together.

00:49:36.710 --> 00:49:39.010
So our time is up.

00:49:39.010 --> 00:49:41.140
And I don't think
there's any better

00:49:41.140 --> 00:49:44.980
way of ending this session
with the very quote

00:49:44.980 --> 00:49:49.030
that Shannon has inspired
me several years ago when

00:49:49.030 --> 00:49:52.690
I was in the audience
listening to one of her talks,

00:49:52.690 --> 00:49:56.170
is that there is no
independent machine values.

00:49:56.170 --> 00:49:58.240
Machine values are human values.

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:02.590
And the technology we
create are eventually

00:50:02.590 --> 00:50:04.030
going to be the
technology that's

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:09.530
going to impact all of us,
our communities, our families,

00:50:09.530 --> 00:50:11.210
and our future generations.

00:50:11.210 --> 00:50:14.920
And we really want to
invite you into this journey

00:50:14.920 --> 00:50:17.800
to develop benevolent
and responsible AI.

00:50:17.800 --> 00:50:18.880
Thank you, so much.

00:50:18.880 --> 00:50:19.480
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:19.480 --> 00:50:21.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

